{
  "_meta": {
    "lastBuilt": "2026-08-31",
    "schemaVersion": 2,
    "purpose": "Named zones on the property. Each zone carries vertices (WGS84 [lon, lat] — schema v2), a status (draft / confirmed / flagged), and a history log of changes. Observations and plants reference zones via the zoneId field. The browse view is itself a thinking tool — looking at the whole property from above helps Mom form mental models of where things are.",
    "coordinateSystem": "wgs84",
    "vertexOrder": "[lon, lat]  — GeoJSON order (x, y). NOT [lat, lon].",
    "schemaNotes": "v2 (2026-07-16): vertices are REAL WGS84 [lon, lat] coordinates. v1 stored fractions of the base image, which meant the record said where a zone APPEARED IN A PICTURE, not where it IS — swap the picture and every polygon moved, and the v1 base was an oblique Google Earth screenshot whose pixels can never be reconciled to a nadir map, so the v1 polygons were unsalvageable. Vertices are now independent of the basemap: a new basemap is a re-registration (update _meta.baseImage + bounds), never a redraw. Zone ids/names are preserved from v1 because plants.json and observations reference them via zoneId.",
    "baseImage": "images/property-map/base-naip-2022-01-leafoff.webp",
    "baseImageFallbackPng": "images/property-map/base-naip-2022-01-leafoff.png",
    "baseImageWidth": 1500,
    "baseImageHeight": 1500,
    "bounds": {
      "west": -84.36990044993301,
      "south": 34.54754054054054,
      "east": -84.364899550067,
      "north": 34.55165945945946
    },
    "boundsNote": "The georeference. Rendering maps each axis LINEARLY and INDEPENDENTLY against its own bound (lon->x, lat->y); the two axes do not share a degrees-per-pixel scale. Across this image the linear-vs-true-Mercator error is sub-centimetre — ~1000x below the imagery's own.",
    "baseImageSource": "USDA NAIP via Microsoft Planetary Computer",
    "baseImageLicense": "public domain (US federal work) — redistributable",
    "baseImageStacItem": "ga_m_3408430_sw_16_060_20220110",
    "baseImageCaptureDate": "2022-01-10",
    "baseImageSeason": "leaf-off",
    "accuracyHonesty": "⚠️ READ THIS BEFORE TREATING ANY BOUNDARY AS A LINE. Paul, on finishing the trace (2026-08-31): 'in this picture it looks like there are a lot of shadows and that made it very hard to be exact with positioning and borders, especially with the low resolution.' That is a REAL and EXPLAINABLE limit, not carelessness — a 10 January capture at 34.5N puts the sun near 32 degrees at noon, so every tree, wall and structure throws a long shadow, and leaf-off bare branches add shadow texture over open ground. At 0.6 m/px there is no detail underneath a shadow to recover. So: these polygons record WHERE A NAME APPLIES, at the resolution of a name. They are NOT survey lines, NOT parcel boundaries, and NOT a basis for anything that turns on where an edge actually falls. The real budget is the NAIP spec (+/-20 ft) plus relief displacement at 2,959 ft plus the shadow ambiguity above — call it worse than +/-30 ft on any edge Paul could not positively see.",
    "lastBuiltAt": "2026-07-17T21:15:09.801Z",
    "fold_2026_08_31": "Mom's own map became canon: 16 areas named by her (2026-08-30 naming session, annotated NAIP aerial) and traced by Paul (2026-08-31, tools/area-trace.html) folded in; fairway + house KEPT per paul-stated rulings; fairway-fringe → fairway-border; parking-bank + upper-uber-wall-area retired (see _deleted). 3 LINEAR features (The Path, Upper-Uber wall, Driveway) are NOT here — the zone-save round-trip rebuilds {_meta, zones} wholesale and would silently drop any other key, so lines stay in the plan file until a schema v3 adds them. FULL provenance, rulings, accuracy budget: .plans/2026-08-31-zones-traced-with-mom.json. CAPTURE TOOLS (do not lose track of these): tools/zone-capture.html (annotation/naming overlay) and tools/area-trace.html (polygon tracing against the registered basemap) — the standalone pages this map was made with; they are NOT the in-app zone editor.",
    "sharedBorders": "Adjacent zones were traced independently, by eye, with no vertex snapping. Neighbouring boundaries WILL disagree by a few feet and leave slivers and overlaps between them. Deferred deliberately (Paul, 2026-08-31): border smoothing and snapping come after the zones exist, not before. Do not read a gap between two zones as unclaimed ground."
  },
